In graphical mode, the text layer is displayed in the background, unless a
backdrop picture is displayed instead. All other things displayed in the
graphical mode are slices, which are made using a slice tree, which is
described by this document.

Each pixel of the screen has a colour and a Z-index. The Z-index is a
8-bit number, and when displaying slices on the screen, the Z-index is used
to determine the priority if there is more than one in the same place. The
higher Z-index has higher priority. If the numbers are equal, then the
existing pixel takes precedence if the Z-index is odd, but the new pixel
value takes precedence if the Z-index is even.

The root slice normally takes up the entire 640x350 screen, and will
usually contain other slices, so it should be a type that can contain
other slices, but may be of any type. Some types of slices are able to
contain other slices, as described below.

There are three "levels" of slices, and there can be a root slice in each
level. The levels of slices are as follows:

* Level 0 is used for the main game. This is used for the screen which is
specified by the board info.

* Level 1 is used for item windows and for the special option menu.

* Level 2 is used for text windows, custom windows, and submenus of the
special option menu.

Each slice has a type, size, optional ID, and any combination of flags:

* NUMBER: Means that this slice has a ID number.

* NOCLIP: Causes the clipping to be ignored for this slice, setting it to
the entire screen (the root slice always does this).

* UNSEEN: The slice is not displayed on the screen (and cannot be used for
graphical collisions). It is still relevant for measuring how much space it
takes up, etc.

* IGNORE: Ignore this slice for most purposes, including measurement,
deciding positioning, etc.

* MANUAL: The metrics are determined manually instead of automatically.

Three more flags are specific to the type of slice, which are called EXTRA1
and EXTRA2 and EXTRA3. Their meanings are described in the below sections.

Some types of slices are "modifier slices". These slices always contain
one other slice, and modifies that slice when it is loaded; the modifier
slice itself does not exist at run time.


=== General controls ===

The bits of the second operand of SLIC have the meanings:

* bit7-bit0 = One of the hexadecimal codes listed below.

* bit8 = Tells whether to use the primary or secondary slice ID. Uses the
primary if this bit is clear or secondary if this bit is set.

[00] Assign the slice ID to the primary slice ID. If this slice does not
have a ID number, then it is assigned ID 1.

[01] Assign the slice ID to the secondary slice ID. If this slice does not
have a ID number, then it is assigned ID 1.

[02] Recalculate the metrics of slices. This one does not set the size
of the slice which is referenced by this operation, but will affect the
sizes of the slices that it contains (if any).

[03] Recalculate the metrics of slices, like 0x01, but it does set the
size of the slice.

[04] If the slice has the MANUAL flag, then it works like code 0x02 but
the MANUAL flag is temporarily cleared. If the slice does not have the
MANUAL flag then it works like 0x03.


=== ID slice [0] ===

This is a modifier slice which gives a 16-bit ID number to another slice.
ID numbers 0 to 3 have built-in uses:

* 0 = The root slice.

* 1 = A number temporarily assigned to a slice that doesn't have a number.

* 2 = (Reserved)

* 3 = (Reserved)

The ASN.1 format is two fields, the first being the ID number (which is a
integer) and the second being the slice being modified.


=== Offset slice [1] ===

This slice contains another slice, as well as X and Y offsets (which can be
positive, negative, or zero). The slice is displayed at the specified
offset relative to where it should naturally be displayed.

The ASN.1 format of this slice is: the X offset, the Y offset, and then the
slice that it contains.


=== Box slice [2] ===

This slice contains zero or more other slices in order, and displays them
next to each other. You can specify the direction it starts and extends in.

The ASN.1 format of this slice is as follows:

* Enumerated specifying the direction: 0 (top to bottom), 1 (bottom to
top), 2 (left to right), 3 (right to left).

* The border colour and Z-index, which is application type 0.

* The fill colour and Z-index, which is application type 0, and it can
include translucency.

* Boolean which is true to add spacing between items or false otherwise.
It may be omitted, in which case it is false. (This is EXTRA2 flag.)

* The margin, which is a signed 8-bit integer.

* Boolean which is true to clip children or false to not clip children.
(This is EXTRA1 flag.)

* Boolean which is true to try to stretch the non-space nodes so that
they will fit equally, or false to use the normal size of non-space nodes
and then resize the box to fit instead. (This is EXTRA3 flag.)

* A sequence of the slices that it contains.


=== Space slice [3] ===

This slice is only meaningful inside of a box slice. It is a space with a
variable size, with the direction of the box that contains it. The size
is specified as the natural size, as well as how much it is allowed to
stretch and how much it is allowed to shrink compared with the natural
size. If the total natural size is too small then any stretchable spaces
are stretched to fit, and if too big then shrinkable spaces will shrink.

The ASN.1 format is three integers, being the natural size, the stretch,
and the shrink.


=== Manual slice [5] ===

This is a modifier slice which sets the MANUAL flag of a slice and sets
the manual width and height.

The ASN.1 format is two integers being the width and height, and then the
slice which is being modified.

